net: tls: keep each TX record in cache lines of its own

On a SoC whose crypto engine does not snoop the CPU caches, kernel TLS
sends records with stale bytes at the end of their ciphertext. The
i.MX6 CAAM does AES-CTR by DMA (gcm(aes) is the gcm template over
ctr-aes-caam with a CPU GHASH), and it is asynchronous: while it writes
record N's ciphertext, tls_sw fills record N+1, whose buffer
sk_msg_alloc() hands out from the same page frag, starting in the cache
line where record N's ciphertext ends. The CPU dirties that line during
the device's write; its write-back, when record N+1's scatterlists are
mapped or the line is evicted, overwrites the last bytes of record N's
ciphertext with what the cache held. GHASH then runs over memory, so
the tag matches the damaged ciphertext: the peer accepts the record and
gets wrong bytes (TLS 1.2), or a wrong inner content type (TLS 1.3,
"bad record type").

Measured on an i.MX6 Solo (Linux 6.12.20, ARCH_DMA_MINALIGN 64): over
AES-128-GCM every damaged range, 3 to 15 bytes, ends exactly at the end
of a record the board sent, never at one it received, and its length
steps with the record length modulo the cache line; ChaCha20-Poly1305,
which runs on the CPU, is byte exact over the same path.

Start every TX record's encrypted buffer on a DMA cache line boundary,
also for the second half of a split record, and move the frag past the
line a record ends in when the record is closed, so that nothing else
is placed in a line the device may still be writing. On a coherent
system dma_get_cache_alignment() is 1 and nothing changes.

Upstream-Status: Pending
Signed-off-by: Scott Wiederhold <s.e.wiederhold@gmail.com>

--- a/net/tls/tls_sw.c
+++ b/net/tls/tls_sw.c
@@ -36,6 +36,7 @@
  */
 
 #include <linux/bug.h>
+#include <linux/dma-mapping.h>
 #include <linux/sched/signal.h>
 #include <linux/module.h>
 #include <linux/kernel.h>
@@ -317,6 +318,28 @@
 	sk_msg_trim(sk, &rec->msg_encrypted, target_size);
 }
 
+/* The AEAD may write a record's ciphertext by DMA from a device that does
+ * not snoop the CPU caches, and it does so while the CPU fills the next
+ * record, which sk_msg_alloc() hands out from the same page frag, right
+ * after this one. A cache line shared by the two is then dirtied by the
+ * CPU during the device's write, and its write-back (when the next
+ * record's buffers are mapped, or the line is evicted) puts stale bytes
+ * over the end of the ciphertext the device wrote. The tag is computed
+ * over what memory holds afterwards, so the record authenticates and
+ * carries the wrong bytes. Start every record's buffer on a DMA cache
+ * line, and move past the line a record ends in once it is closed, so
+ * that nothing else is placed there while it is encrypted.
+ */
+static void tls_align_page_frag(struct sock *sk)
+{
+	struct page_frag *pfrag = sk_page_frag(sk);
+	unsigned int align = dma_get_cache_alignment();
+
+	if (align > 1 && pfrag->page)
+		pfrag->offset = min_t(u32, ALIGN(pfrag->offset, align),
+				      pfrag->size);
+}
+
 static int tls_alloc_encrypted_msg(struct sock *sk, int len)
 {
 	struct tls_context *tls_ctx = tls_get_ctx(sk);
@@ -324,6 +347,8 @@
 	struct tls_rec *rec = ctx->open_rec;
 	struct sk_msg *msg_en = &rec->msg_encrypted;
 
+	if (!msg_en->sg.size)
+		tls_align_page_frag(sk);
 	return sk_msg_alloc(sk, msg_en, len, 0);
 }
 
@@ -619,6 +644,7 @@
 	new = tls_get_rec(sk);
 	if (!new)
 		return -ENOMEM;
+	tls_align_page_frag(sk);
 	ret = sk_msg_alloc(sk, &new->msg_encrypted, msg_opl->sg.size +
 			   tx_overhead_size, 0);
 	if (ret < 0) {
@@ -815,6 +841,11 @@
 			 record_type);
 
 	tls_ctx->pending_open_record_frags = false;
+
+	/* The record is closed: what the frag hands out next starts on a
+	 * line of its own (see tls_align_page_frag()).
+	 */
+	tls_align_page_frag(sk);
 
 	rc = tls_do_encryption(sk, tls_ctx, ctx, req,
 			       msg_pl->sg.size + prot->tail_size, i);
